{"text": "Olivia learns most successfully through accessible digital text and keyboard shortcuts; frequent position changes and planned rest; choice of oral, typed, or recorded response; and materials prepared before class transitions. These strategies make instruction clearer and reduce the amount of energy spent simply gaining access.\n\nThe profile also identifies predictable barriers. Olivia may struggle with fatigue during long blocks; inaccessible tables, sinks, and lab stations; writing volume, not idea generation; and missed instruction during personal-care routines. We should not treat these points as a list of personal deficits. Each one directs the team toward a response. For example, a barrier caused by pace may require additional processing time; an environmental barrier may require a quieter, safer, or more accessible setting; and task complexity may require modeling, chunking, direct instruction, or an alternate response.\n\nThe goal is not to remove every productive challenge. The goal is to remove barriers that prevent the student from engaging with the intended learning. Presenter 3 will now connect the profile’s sensory, medical, behavioural, and safety information to the EA’s day-to-day responsibilities.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-A-04.mp3", "words": [{"text": "Olivia", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.5625, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 6}, {"text": "learns", "start": 0.575, "end": 0.925, "start_char": 7, "end_char": 13}, {"text": "most", "start": 0.9375, "end": 1.2, "start_char": 14, "end_char": 18}, {"text": "successfully", "start": 1.2125, "end": 1.8875, "start_char": 19, "end_char": 31}, {"text": "through", "start": 1.9, "end": 2.1125, "start_char": 32, "end_char": 39}, {"text": "accessible", "start": 2.125, "end": 2.725, "start_char": 40, "end_char": 50}, {"text": "digital", "start": 2.7375, "end": 3.15, "start_char": 51, "end_char": 58}, {"text": "text", "start": 3.1625, "end": 3.5875, "start_char": 59, "end_char":